1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,640
A calibration graph turns an absorbance into a

2
00:00:02,720 --> 00:00:06,240
concentration, but exam questions add a twist.

3
00:00:06,720 --> 00:00:08,880
The sample reads above the standards.

4
00:00:09,040 --> 00:00:10,880
The line doesn't start at zero.

5
00:00:11,280 --> 00:00:13,600
Or the result is right, but it's from

6
00:00:13,640 --> 00:00:16,079
the wrong sample for the question being asked.

7
00:00:16,640 --> 00:00:20,480
Here are three HSC-style questions on water samples.

8
00:00:20,720 --> 00:00:23,040
Pause and try each one, then we'll work

9
00:00:23,120 --> 00:00:24,000
through it together.

10
00:00:25,200 --> 00:00:26,080
Question one.

11
00:00:26,640 --> 00:00:30,400
Copper standards are measured by atomic absorption spectroscopy,

12
00:00:30,440 --> 00:00:32,080
and the readings are on screen.

13
00:00:32,720 --> 00:00:34,960
A sample of mine drainage water gives an

14
00:00:35,080 --> 00:00:37,840
absorbance of point six two zero.

15
00:00:37,880 --> 00:00:40,240
The student then dilutes ten millilitres of it

16
00:00:40,320 --> 00:00:43,520
to fifty millilitres, and the diluted solution reads

17
00:00:43,680 --> 00:00:45,400
point one four two.

18
00:00:45,760 --> 00:00:49,040
Construct the calibration graph, explain why the sample

19
00:00:49,120 --> 00:00:52,320
was diluted, and calculate the copper concentration in

20
00:00:52,360 --> 00:00:53,520
the drainage water.

21
00:00:53,840 --> 00:00:55,280
Pause here to answer.

22
00:01:05,700 --> 00:01:06,420
Part a.

23
00:01:06,500 --> 00:01:10,020
The standards' concentration was chosen, so it goes

24
00:01:10,100 --> 00:01:12,660
along the bottom, with absorbance up the side.

25
00:01:13,300 --> 00:01:16,020
Use even steps that fill the grid, label

26
00:01:16,100 --> 00:01:18,900
both axes with units, and plot each point

27
00:01:18,980 --> 00:01:20,180
as a small cross.

28
00:01:20,740 --> 00:01:23,300
Then rule one straight line of best fit

29
00:01:23,380 --> 00:01:26,420
through the points, including the blank, not dot

30
00:01:26,500 --> 00:01:26,980
to dot.

31
00:01:27,620 --> 00:01:30,260
These points sit almost exactly on it, and

32
00:01:30,340 --> 00:01:32,580
the slope is about point one absorbance for

33
00:01:32,660 --> 00:01:34,180
each milligram per litre.

34
00:01:35,400 --> 00:01:36,120
Part b.

35
00:01:36,200 --> 00:01:39,080
The top standard reads about point four.

36
00:01:39,560 --> 00:01:42,840
The undiluted sample, at point six two zero,

37
00:01:42,960 --> 00:01:45,640
is well above it, outside the standards.

38
00:01:45,960 --> 00:01:48,040
Nothing in this data shows the line stays

39
00:01:48,200 --> 00:01:51,560
straight up there, and atomic absorption calibrations often

40
00:01:51,640 --> 00:01:53,640
bend at high concentration.

41
00:01:53,840 --> 00:01:55,960
So the student diluted it, to bring the

42
00:01:56,040 --> 00:01:58,680
reading back inside the range the standards cover.

43
00:01:59,900 --> 00:02:00,780
Part c.

44
00:02:01,260 --> 00:02:03,420
Read across from point one four two to

45
00:02:03,500 --> 00:02:06,140
the line, then down, and show those read-off

46
00:02:06,220 --> 00:02:09,580
lines: one point four two milligrams per litre.

47
00:02:09,980 --> 00:02:11,500
That's the diluted solution.

48
00:02:12,060 --> 00:02:14,300
Fifty over ten is a dilution factor of

49
00:02:14,380 --> 00:02:17,180
five, so the drainage water holds about seven

50
00:02:17,340 --> 00:02:19,260
point one milligrams per litre.

51
00:02:20,467 --> 00:02:22,706
The classic slip is extending the line up

52
00:02:22,787 --> 00:02:24,626
to point six two zero.

53
00:02:24,867 --> 00:02:27,586
That gives about six point two, nearly one

54
00:02:27,667 --> 00:02:31,426
milligram per litre below the dilution-corrected result, because

55
00:02:31,507 --> 00:02:34,066
the real response bends below the straight line

56
00:02:34,147 --> 00:02:34,626
up there.

57
00:02:35,107 --> 00:02:37,506
Outside the standards, doubt the line.

58
00:02:37,827 --> 00:02:39,426
Dilute and remeasure.

59
00:02:40,634 --> 00:02:41,513
Question two.

60
00:02:42,074 --> 00:02:45,193
Iron in bore water is measured by colourimetry.

61
00:02:45,754 --> 00:02:48,473
A reagent turns the iron into an orange-red

62
00:02:48,554 --> 00:02:51,113
complex, and the same amount of reagent goes

63
00:02:51,194 --> 00:02:53,753
into every standard and into the sample.

64
00:02:54,394 --> 00:02:56,953
The instrument was zeroed with distilled water.

65
00:02:57,514 --> 00:02:59,753
The calibration line and its equation are on

66
00:02:59,834 --> 00:03:02,633
screen, and the bore water reads point two

67
00:03:02,714 --> 00:03:03,513
nine zero.

68
00:03:04,154 --> 00:03:07,353
Explain why the zero standard has an absorbance,

69
00:03:07,434 --> 00:03:10,153
then calculate the iron concentration in the bore

70
00:03:10,234 --> 00:03:10,633
water.

71
00:03:11,194 --> 00:03:12,473
Pause here to answer.

72
00:03:22,900 --> 00:03:23,620
Part a.

73
00:03:23,780 --> 00:03:27,140
The zero standard has no added iron, but

74
00:03:27,180 --> 00:03:29,540
it still reads point zero three two.

75
00:03:30,020 --> 00:03:32,500
It does contain the reagent, and the instrument

76
00:03:32,580 --> 00:03:34,820
was zeroed with plain water, not with the

77
00:03:34,900 --> 00:03:35,460
reagent.

78
00:03:36,020 --> 00:03:38,820
So something in the reagent absorbs a little,

79
00:03:38,980 --> 00:03:40,740
perhaps a trace of iron in the chemicals

80
00:03:40,820 --> 00:03:41,860
themselves.

81
00:03:42,180 --> 00:03:44,980
Every standard and the sample carry that same

82
00:03:45,140 --> 00:03:47,380
extra point zero three two.

83
00:03:47,460 --> 00:03:49,540
That's why the line starts above zero.

84
00:03:50,767 --> 00:03:51,406
Part b.

85
00:03:51,487 --> 00:03:54,246
Take the reagent's share off first.

86
00:03:54,527 --> 00:03:57,246
Point two nine zero minus point zero three

87
00:03:57,327 --> 00:03:59,726
two is point two five eight.

88
00:03:59,807 --> 00:04:02,206
That's the absorbance from the bore water's iron.

89
00:04:02,687 --> 00:04:05,486
Divide by the slope, point one nine nine,

90
00:04:05,567 --> 00:04:07,566
and you get about one point three zero

91
00:04:07,727 --> 00:04:08,926
milligrams per litre.

92
00:04:09,487 --> 00:04:11,566
Reading across to the line and down gives

93
00:04:11,647 --> 00:04:12,606
the same answer.

94
00:04:13,834 --> 00:04:16,553
The classic slip is dividing point two nine

95
00:04:16,714 --> 00:04:18,833
zero by the slope straight away.

96
00:04:19,114 --> 00:04:21,753
That gives about one point four six, too

97
00:04:21,834 --> 00:04:24,633
high, because it counts the reagent's absorbance as

98
00:04:24,874 --> 00:04:25,553
iron.

99
00:04:25,834 --> 00:04:28,473
When a line has an intercept, subtract it

100
00:04:28,714 --> 00:04:30,073
before you divide.

101
00:04:30,194 --> 00:04:32,793
Better still, zero the instrument with a reagent

102
00:04:32,954 --> 00:04:35,993
blank, a cuvette holding every reagent but no

103
00:04:36,154 --> 00:04:36,713
iron.

104
00:04:37,934 --> 00:04:38,893
Question three.

105
00:04:39,374 --> 00:04:41,613
A school tests the lead in water from

106
00:04:41,694 --> 00:04:42,733
one drinking bubbler.

107
00:04:43,294 --> 00:04:45,533
A sample taken first thing in the morning,

108
00:04:45,694 --> 00:04:48,013
after the water stood in the pipes overnight,

109
00:04:48,294 --> 00:04:51,453
contains point zero one two milligrams per litre.

110
00:04:52,174 --> 00:04:54,253
A sample taken after the bubbler ran for

111
00:04:54,334 --> 00:04:57,853
two minutes contains point zero zero two.

112
00:04:57,934 --> 00:04:59,693
The school claims the water is safe to

113
00:04:59,774 --> 00:05:02,093
drink, because the flushed sample is under the

114
00:05:02,174 --> 00:05:03,213
guideline value.

115
00:05:03,694 --> 00:05:04,973
Evaluate this claim.

116
00:05:05,534 --> 00:05:06,893
Pause here to answer.

117
00:05:17,300 --> 00:05:18,660
Start with the method.

118
00:05:18,900 --> 00:05:20,420
Can it measure lead at this level?

119
00:05:20,900 --> 00:05:24,580
Its validated quantification limit, the lowest concentration this

120
00:05:24,660 --> 00:05:27,540
method can measure with the stated reliability, is

121
00:05:27,620 --> 00:05:30,580
point zero zero one, five times below the

122
00:05:30,660 --> 00:05:32,260
guideline, so yes.

123
00:05:32,980 --> 00:05:34,180
Now the morning sample.

124
00:05:34,660 --> 00:05:37,300
Point zero one two over point zero zero

125
00:05:37,500 --> 00:05:39,860
five is about two point four times the

126
00:05:39,940 --> 00:05:40,580
guideline.

127
00:05:40,980 --> 00:05:42,740
The flushed sample is under it.

128
00:05:43,220 --> 00:05:45,700
So lead is probably dissolving into the water

129
00:05:45,780 --> 00:05:47,780
while it stands in the pipes and fittings.

130
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:49,960
Now the claim.

131
00:05:50,440 --> 00:05:53,000
The flushed result is real, but it isn't

132
00:05:53,080 --> 00:05:54,760
the water many students drink.

133
00:05:55,320 --> 00:05:57,240
The first drink of the day comes after

134
00:05:57,320 --> 00:06:00,120
the water has stood overnight, and that sample

135
00:06:00,280 --> 00:06:01,640
was over the guideline.

136
00:06:02,200 --> 00:06:04,680
And one bubbler on one day doesn't show

137
00:06:04,760 --> 00:06:06,120
what the others do.

138
00:06:06,240 --> 00:06:08,520
So the claim isn't supported.

139
00:06:08,560 --> 00:06:11,240
The school needs repeat samples from every bubbler

140
00:06:11,320 --> 00:06:13,960
over several days, and advice from the local

141
00:06:14,040 --> 00:06:16,520
public health unit to find and control the

142
00:06:16,600 --> 00:06:17,400
lead source.

143
00:06:18,600 --> 00:06:21,480
The trap is averaging the two results.

144
00:06:21,560 --> 00:06:24,120
Point zero zero seven sounds like a fair

145
00:06:24,200 --> 00:06:27,960
middle, but the two samples answer different questions:

146
00:06:28,040 --> 00:06:30,600
one is the water after it stands, the

147
00:06:30,680 --> 00:06:32,680
other after it runs.

148
00:06:32,800 --> 00:06:36,840
Their average describes no water anyone actually drinks.

149
00:06:36,960 --> 00:06:38,760
Match the sample to the question.

150
00:06:39,967 --> 00:06:43,446
So, for any calibration or monitoring question.

151
00:06:43,727 --> 00:06:46,686
Read the unknown only inside the standards, and

152
00:06:46,727 --> 00:06:48,766
dilute it if it's above them.

153
00:06:49,047 --> 00:06:51,486
Multiply back by every dilution.

154
00:06:51,567 --> 00:06:53,966
If the line has an intercept, subtract it

155
00:06:54,047 --> 00:06:55,486
before you divide.

156
00:06:55,607 --> 00:06:57,406
And judge a result with the sample that

157
00:06:57,607 --> 00:07:01,006
matches the question, which water, when and where,

158
00:07:01,087 --> 00:07:03,486
and not from one sample alone.

159
00:07:03,887 --> 00:07:07,406
Next: why monitor water and nutrient pollution?

160
00:07:08,634 --> 00:07:10,073
Here are the key notes.

161
00:07:10,314 --> 00:07:12,233
Pause the video if you need more time

162
00:07:12,314 --> 00:07:13,113
to copy them.
